1. Aim
To compare the performance of String, StringBuilder and StringBuffer in generating railway e-tickets under different workloads.
2. Prediction
- String is expected to take more execution time because it creates new objects during repeated concatenation.
- StringBuilder is expected to perform faster because it modifies the same object.
- StringBuffer may take slightly more time than StringBuilder because its methods are synchronized.
3. Experimental Workloads
Workload	Number of tickets	Fields per ticket
Small	1	5
Medium	1,000	20
Large	10,000	50

4. Observed Results
Your actual results so far:
Workload	String	StringBuilder	StringBuffer
Small	0.017 ms	0.002 ms	0.003 ms
Medium	19.276 ms	2.301 ms	13.570 ms
Large	Pending	Pending	Pending

5. Analysis
The experimental results show that StringBuilder performs faster than String and StringBuffer in the small and medium workloads.

String creates intermediate objects because it is immutable. StringBuilder provides better performance because it modifies the existing character sequence. StringBuffer provides thread-safe operations through synchronization, which may increase execution time.
6. Conclusion
StringBuilder is suitable for generating individual railway e-tickets because it provides better performance. StringBuffer can be considered for a shared log when multiple threads modify the same buffer, provided access is properly synchronized.
